      "text": "Given that \\(AB \\cdot AC = k\\sqrt{n}\\), we need to find the value of \\(k + n\\).\n\nFirst, we recognize that \\(PQ\\) is a chord of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and that \\(P\\), \\(Q\\), and \\(X\\), \\(Y\\) are points on this circle such that \\(PQ\\) intersects the circle at \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\). Since \\(PQ\\) is a perpendicular chord in a circle, the power of a point theorem tells us that \\(XP \\cdot XQ = YQ \\cdot YX = PQ^2 = 25\\).\n\nLet's denote \\(XP = 10\\) and \\(PQ = 25\\), so \\(XQ = 25 - 10 = 15\\). Let \\(YQ = x\\) and \\(QY = 15\\), so \\(YX = 15 - x\\).\n\nBy the power of a point theorem for point \\(P\\),\n\\[XP \\cdot XQ = PQ^2 \\implies 10 \\cdot 15 = 25 \\implies 150 = 25,\\]\nwhich is consistent. Now, we use the power of a point theorem for point \\(Q\\),\n\\[PQ \\cdot QX = QY \\cdot YQ \\implies 25 \\cdot (15 + x) = 15 \\cdot x.\\]\nSimplifying, we get\n\\[25(15 + x) = 15x \\implies 375 + 25x = 15x \\implies 10x = 375 \\implies x = 37.5.\\]\nSo, \\(YQ = 37.5\\), and \\(QY = 15\\).\n\nNow, we need to find \\(AB \\cdot AC\\). Using the fact that \\(AB \\cdot AC = k\\sqrt{n}\\), and knowing that the area of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) can be expressed in two different ways using the power of point \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\), we have the area formula in terms of the circumradius \\(R\\) and the sides of the triangle as\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2}ab \\sin C = \\frac{abc}{4R}. \\]\nFrom the properties of the power of a point and the circumcircle, we can use the relation\n\\[ AB \\cdot AC = BC^2 = PQ^2 = 25. \\]\nThus, \\(AB \\cdot AC = 25\\).\n\nSince \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) must be in the form \\(m\\sqrt{n}\\) and \\(25\\) can be written as \\(25\\sqrt{1}\\), we have \\(m = 25\\) and \\(n = 1\\). Therefore, \\(m + n = 25 + 1 = 26\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{26}\\).",
