      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by noting the given information and the properties of the triangle and its circumcircle. We know that \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(C\\) to \\(AB\\) and from \\(B\\) to \\(AC\\) respectively. This means that \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are symmetric with respect to the angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAC\\). The line \\(PQ\\) is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle ABC\\).\n\nSince \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are the points where the Simson line intersects the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\), we know that \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are the reflections of \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) over the midpoint of \\(BC\\) (the point where the altitude from \\(B\\) meets \\(AC\\)) in the circumcircle. Therefore, \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are the points such that \\(PX = PY\\) and \\(QX = QY\\).\n\nGiven that \\(XP = 10\\), \\(PQ = 25\\), and \\(QY = 15\\), we need to find the value of \\(AB \\cdot AC\\). By the properties of the Simson line, we know that the power of point \\(P\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is equal to \\(PX \\cdot PY = PQ \\cdot PQ = 625\\). Similarly, the power of point \\(Q\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is equal to \\(QX \\cdot QY = PQ \\cdot PQ = 625\\).\n\nLet \\(AB = c\\) and \\(AC = b\\). Then, by the power of point \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) with respect to the circumcircle, we have:\n\\[ PX \\cdot PY = PQ^2 = 625 \\]\n\\[ QX \\cdot QY = PQ^2 = 625 \\]\n\nSince \\(PX = 10\\) and \\(PY = PQ^2 - PX = 625 - 10 = 615\\), we need to find \\(AB \\cdot AC\\). The product \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) corresponds to the power of the circumcenter with respect to the triangle, which is given by the formula for the product of the sides of a triangle in terms of the circumradius \\(R\\).\n\nHowever, a simpler approach is to use the fact that \\(AB \\cdot AC = BC \\cdot CA = PA \\cdot PB = QD \\cdot QE\\), where \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(C\\) and \\(B\\) respectively. This product is equal to the power of point \\(P\\) times the power of point \\(Q\\) divided by the area of the triangle, but a simpler direct calculation gives us:\n\\[ AB \\cdot AC = \\frac{PY \\cdot PQ}{\\sin \\angle BPQ} = \\frac{615 \\cdot 25}{\\sin \\angle BPQ} = \\frac{15375}{\\sin \\angle BPQ} \\]\nGiven the symmetry and the properties of the circumcircle, we can directly use the power of points to find:\n\\[ AB \\cdot AC = \\frac{625}{\\sin \\angle BPQ} = \\frac{625}{\\sin \\angle BPQ} = \\frac{625}{\\sin 90^\\circ} = \\frac{625}{1} = 625 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) is \\(1000\\), and the sum \\(m+n\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1000} \\]",
