      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by noting that \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(C\\) to \\(\\overline{AB}\\) and from \\(B\\) to \\(\\overline{AC}\\) respectively, making \\(\\triangle PQC\\) and \\(\\triangle BQC\\) right triangles. The line \\(PQ\\) intersects the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) at points \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\(XP = 10\\)\n- \\(PQ = 25\\)\n- \\(QY = 15\\)\n\nWe need to find the value of \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) in the form \\(m\\sqrt{n}\\) and determine \\(m+n\\).\n\nFirst, observe that \\(XP\\) and \\(QY\\) are segments of \\(PQ\\). Since \\(PQ = 25\\), we have:\n\\[ XP + PQ - PQ = XP = 10 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad XQ + PQ - PQ = QY = 15. \\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[ XQ = PQ - PQ + PQ = 25 - 25 = 15. \\]\n\nNow, we use the fact that \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are points where the line \\(PQ\\) intersects the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\). By properties of cyclic quadrilaterals and the fact that \\(PQ\\) is a chord of the circumcircle, we know that:\n\\[ XP \\cdot XQ = XY \\cdot YX. \\]\n\nSince \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(PQ\\), we have:\n\\[ XY = PQ = 25. \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ XP \\cdot XQ = XY \\cdot YX \\implies 10 \\cdot 15 = 25 \\cdot 25 \\implies 150 = 625/3. \\]\n\nHowever, we need to find the product \\(AB \\cdot AC\\). By the Power of a Point theorem, we have:\n\\[ XP \\cdot XQ = AB \\cdot AC. \\]\n\nSince \\(XP = 10\\) and \\(XQ = 15\\), we have:\n\\[ AB \\cdot AC = 10 \\cdot 15 = 150. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) is \\(150\\), which can be written in the form \\(m\\sqrt{n}\\) as \\(150 \\sqrt{1}\\). Therefore, \\(m = 150\\) and \\(n = 1\\), and the value of \\(m+n\\) is:\n\\[ m+n = 150+1 = 151. \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{151}\n\\]",
